id: pm-li2fj5
title: "Episode substrate: a portable episode specification plus an SDK control surface that composes recipe, snapshot, bounded observation and computed verdict into one reproducible unit of work"
description: "pm-nfrhf0 names four things an agent training or evaluation loop needs and states plainly that pm nearly has all four and that what is missing is the composition. Each of the four now has an owner: pm-dkrmzv owns the workspace snapshot boundary, pm-rbcvt2 owns deterministic construction from a declared recipe, pm-e96opw owns computed per-criterion verdicts, and the bounded task projection is served by the existing read surface with pm-cxr0jb narrowing it to a declared intent. Nothing owns the composition itself, and the composition is the part an environment author would otherwise write from scratch every time.\n\nThe missing artifact is an episode: a named, bounded, reproducible unit of work with a declared beginning, a declared observation, a declared success condition, and a recorded end. Concretely it is two things.\n\nFirst, a portable episode specification. A declared artifact that names the workspace recipe to construct from, the item or item set that forms the task, which fields the agent may observe and which are withheld for grading, the token budget the observation must fit, the criteria whose verdicts decide the outcome, and the limits -- wall clock, mutation count, tool budget -- that bound the run. This is distinct from pm-rbcvt2's recipe, which declares how a workspace is constructed and stops there. The specification declares what is asked of the agent and how the answer is judged, and it must be storable, shareable and re-executable by a different machine and a different pm build, exactly as a recipe is.\n\nSecond, an episode control surface in the public SDK. Open an episode from a specification and obtain an isolated instance. Read the current observation as a bounded projection. Let the agent act through the ordinary pm surface with no episode-specific API, because an environment that requires special calls is not measuring the tool anyone actually uses. Score the episode by evaluating the declared criteria against recorded state. Close it and obtain a result. The whole cycle must be reachable through the SDK alone, per pm-nfrhf0's own criterion that an environment can embed pm without shelling out.\n\nThree properties matter more than the surface shape.\n\nThe grader must read only recorded state. If scoring consults anything the history does not contain, the result is not reproducible and the episode is not a verifier. This is what makes pm a substrate rather than scaffolding: reward is a function of the immutable record.\n\nThe agent must not be able to reach the grading fields. Withholding is a projection concern and belongs in the observation contract, not in an honour system. An episode where the success condition is readable is an episode that measures nothing.\n\nThe same loop must serve four workloads without special paths, because they differ only in what varies. A benchmark fixes the specification and varies the agent. An evaluation fixes the agent and varies the specification. Training varies both and consumes many episodes. A simulation-to-reality transfer varies the workspace scale between a synthetic instance and a real one, which is exactly the recipe-versus-real-workspace axis pm-rbcvt2 already draws. If the surface needs a different shape for any of the four, it is the wrong surface.\n\nScale boundaries are the same ones the rest of the project holds itself to. A single item and a single episode must work with no special path, and concurrent episodes must not share mutable state, so a fleet can run thousands at once against isolated instances.\n\nThis item owns the composition and the specification format. It does not own snapshots, determinism, criterion verdicts, or observation projection, all of which are declared dependencies with existing owners. It does not own the dataset produced by running episodes, which is its own item."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 1
tags[8]: "area:sdk",benchmark,context-management,determinism,eval,rl,training,universal-domain
